From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jrash <johnrash@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 10:53:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 057/342] usb_audio: survive unplugging and replugging while in
 use

Unplugged while the media_server used it, the device stopped its
transfers, and the buffer exchange waited for them forever: the media
node using the device as its time source stopped its clock, and the
mixer and players depending on it died in
"BTimeSource::RealTimeFor: performance time too large". Replugged, the
endpoints were set up again by creating a second set of streams next to
the old ones, whose pipes were gone; the exchange hung again.

* Removal wakes up a waiting buffer exchange. While the device is gone,
  the exchange goes on in real time, the streams' buffers in turn, as if
  they were played and recorded: clocks keep going.
* A replugged device keeps its streams (the client has their buffers
  mapped); only their pipes are renewed, the streams start again with
  the next exchange, and the sampling rate is set again (the device
  forgot it).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
